Rem ==================================================================
Rem str.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: strings
Rem Built on the Instr / InstrFrom / Left / Right / Mid / Len builtins.
Rem Pure.
Rem Three families: surgery (Split .. StrRep), boolean conversions
Rem (IsBool / ToBool / ToBoolOr over the primitive ToBoolOpt — the
Rem string-to-number side lives in the IsNumeric / Val / ValOr builtins),
Rem and fixed-width formatting (PadLeft / PadRight / PadZero / ToFixed /
Rem Commas).
Rem ==================================================================

Rem Split carries a position rather than a shrinking string. It used to
Rem recurse on Mid(s, k + Len(sep), Len(s)) — a fresh copy of the whole
Rem tail per field — because Instr can only search from the beginning, so
Rem splitting into n fields copied the subject n times and Split was
Rem quadratic. InstrFrom searches from a position without copying to it,
Rem which leaves only the field itself to copy: the same answers, in
Rem linear time, for the most-used word in the library.
Rem
Rem The refusal stays in Split and stays first, so Split(s, "") errors
Rem before any scanning happens, exactly as it did.
Def Split(s As String, sep As String) As List Of String
    If Len(sep) = 0 Then
        Error("SPLIT: EMPTY SEPARATOR")
    Else
        SplitFrom(s, sep, 1)

Def SplitFrom(s As String, sep As String, i As Number) As List Of String
    SplitAt(s, sep, i, InstrFrom(s, sep, i))

Rem A position past the end is not a mistake here: the separator ending
Rem the subject leaves an empty last field, and Mid answers "" for it.
Def SplitAt(s As String, sep As String, i As Number, k As Number) As List Of String
    If k = 0 Then
        { Mid(s, i, Len(s) - i + 1) }
    Else
        Prepend(Mid(s, i, k - i), SplitFrom(s, sep, k + Len(sep)))

Def Join(xs As List Of String, sep As String) As String
    If IsEmpty(xs) Then
        ""
    Else
        Fold(Rest(xs), First(xs), Fn(a, x) => a & sep & x)

Def Trim(s As String) As String
    If Len(s) = 0 Then
        s
    Else
        If Left(s, 1) = " " Then
            Trim(Mid(s, 2, Len(s)))
        Else
            If Right(s, 1) = " " Then
                Trim(Left(s, Len(s) - 1))
            Else
                s

Rem Replace gets the same treatment and for the same reason: it recursed
Rem on a copy of the tail, so every substitution copied the rest of the
Rem subject before searching it. Carrying the position removes that copy
Rem entirely.
Rem
Rem It does NOT make Replace linear, and Split's comment above should not
Rem be read as saying so. What remains is the & chain — each level joins
Rem its piece onto the whole result of the levels below it, which is
Rem quadratic in the number of substitutions and cannot be otherwise
Rem while & is how strings are assembled. Removing the tail copy roughly
Rem halves the work; making the assembly linear is a different change and
Rem would need a word this library does not have.
Def Replace(s As String, old As String, new As String) As String
    If Len(old) = 0 Then
        Error("REPLACE: EMPTY PATTERN")
    Else
        ReplaceFrom(s, old, new, 1)

Def ReplaceFrom(s As String, old As String, new As String, i As Number) As String
    ReplaceAt(s, old, new, i, InstrFrom(s, old, i))

Def ReplaceAt(s As String, old As String, new As String, i As Number, k As Number) As String
    If k = 0 Then
        Mid(s, i, Len(s) - i + 1)
    Else
        Mid(s, i, k - i) & new & ReplaceFrom(s, old, new, k + Len(old))

Def StartsWith(s As String, p As String) As Boolean
    Left(s, Len(p)) = p

Def EndsWith(s As String, p As String) As Boolean
    Right(s, Len(p)) = p

Def StrRep(s As String, n As Number) As String
    If n <= 0 Then
        ""
    Else
        s & StrRep(s, n - 1)

Rem ---- conversions -----------------------------------------------------
Rem The boolean side of the guard family, named to match the number side
Rem exactly: IsNumeric / Val / ValOr are builtins, and IsBool / ToBool /
Rem ToBoolOr do the same three offices for booleans. ToBool is strict —
Rem TRUE or FALSE, any case, outer space ignored — while ToBoolOr and
Rem ToBoolOpt also accept the prompt vocabulary: TRUE/T/YES/Y/1 and
Rem FALSE/F/NO/N/0.
Rem
Rem ToBoolOpt is the primitive and the other two are one-liners over it.
Rem That is the whole point of Option: a strict word throws the answer
Rem away on failure and a fallback word throws away the fact that there
Rem WAS a failure, so neither can be built from the other — but both are
Rem built from this. Option is the language's, not a machine's, so no
Rem Include is needed to match on what this returns.
Rem
Rem These were Bool and BoolOr until v1.7.0. BoolOr in particular read as
Rem a logic gate and was in fact a parser, and the Bool* prefix belonged
Rem to bit and boolean work rather than to string conversion.

Def IsBool(s As String) As Boolean
    Let u = Upper(Trim(s))
    u = "TRUE" Or u = "FALSE"

Def ToBoolOpt(s As String) As Option
    Let u = Upper(Trim(s))
    If u = "TRUE" Or u = "T" Or u = "YES" Or u = "Y" Or u = "1" Then
        Some(True)
    Else
        If u = "FALSE" Or u = "F" Or u = "NO" Or u = "N" Or u = "0" Then
            Some(False)
        Else
            None()

Def ToBool(s As String) As Boolean
    If IsBool(s) Then
        Upper(Trim(s)) = "TRUE"
    Else
        Error("TOBOOL: '" & s & "' is not TRUE or FALSE")

Def ToBoolOr(s As String, fallback As Boolean) As Boolean
    Select Case ToBoolOpt(s)
        Case Some(b)
            b
        Case None
            fallback

Rem ---- formatting ------------------------------------------------------
Rem Fixed-width fields for monospace readouts. Every mill was growing its
Rem own pads and decimal formatter; these are the library versions.

Rem s padded with spaces to width n; unchanged if already that wide.
Def PadLeft(s As String, n As Number) As String
    StrRep(" ", n - Len(s)) & s

Def PadRight(s As String, n As Number) As String
    s & StrRep(" ", n - Len(s))

Rem x as a whole number zero-padded to at least n digits ("07"); a minus
Rem sign rides outside the padding.
Def PadZero(x As Number, n As Number) As String
    If x < 0 Then
        "-" & PadZero(0 - x, n)
    Else
        Let d = Str(Round(x))
        StrRep("0", n - Len(d)) & d

Rem x with exactly p decimal places — "0.40", never "0.4" — rounded
Rem half-up at the last shown digit. p = 0 is the whole-number
Rem formatter: no decimal point.
Def ToFixed(x As Number, p As Number) As String
    If p <= 0 Then
        Str(Round(x))
    Else
        Let s = 10 ^ p
        Let n = Round(x * s)
        Let a = Abs(n)
        Ifte(n < 0, "-", "") & Str(Fix(a / s)) & "." & PadZero(a Mod s, p)

Rem A digit string grouped in threes from the right: "1234567" becomes
Rem "1,234,567". Commas' helper; digits only, no sign.
Def CommaGroup(digits As String) As String
    If Len(digits) <= 3 Then
        digits
    Else
        CommaGroup(Left(digits, Len(digits) - 3)) & "," & Right(digits, 3)

Rem The classic ##,###.## — x with thousands commas and p decimals.
Def Commas(x As Number, p As Number) As String
    Let q = Max(p, 0)
    Let s = 10 ^ q
    Let n = Round(x * s)
    Let a = Abs(n)
    Let head = Ifte(n < 0, "-", "") & CommaGroup(Str(Fix(a / s)))
    If q = 0 Then
        head
    Else
        head & "." & PadZero(a Mod s, q)
